<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl mb-8">Why switch from what you run now</h2>
  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-x-10 gap-y-10">
    <article>
      <h3 class="text-lg mb-2">Already running KlipperScreen?</h3>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted mb-3">
        Happy Hare and AFC get their own interface, so an ERCF or Box Turtle shows its
        filament path, the spool in each slot and buffer health on the touchscreen. Each
        spool's remaining weight is tracked as it prints.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        The home screen is a grid of widgets you arrange yourself, in any of 18 themes, with
        layouts of its own for portrait and ultrawide displays. It needs about 15 MB of RAM
        against KlipperScreen's 50, and no X11.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3 class="text-lg mb-2">Printer came with its own screen?</h3>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted mb-3">
        Factory screens don't support Spoolman. HelixScreen does, and it runs the
        multi-material units those printers ship with: Creality CFS, the AD5X's IFS and
        Anycubic ACE.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Klipper and Moonraker stay as they are. Only the screen changes.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3 class="text-lg mb-2">Worth the trouble?</h3>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted mb-3">
        One command installs it and stops KlipperScreen for you. On first launch it detects
        the printer and its hardware, so there is little to configure.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        If it isn't for you, <code class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">--uninstall</code> puts
        back the screen you had before, stock or KlipperScreen. <a href="/installation/" class="text-secondary underline underline-offset-2">The install guide</a> has the details for each printer.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
